Moth checks the source type and the target type to decide whether they
support a cast. For builtin numbers, the two types also tell you whether
the cast can fail.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    widen |value U8| -> U16 {
        return cast value
    }

    narrow |value U16| -> U8 {
        return cast value catch then 0
    }

    wide = widen(255)
    small = narrow(300)
]

Every `U8` fits in a `U16`, so `wide` holds 255. The reverse cast needs a
range check. The second call passes 300, which exceeds the largest `U8`,
so `small` holds the fallback of zero.

For a struct or choice, the compiler looks for an explicit promise that its
receiver method supplies the conversion. See
@../traits/#core-cast-traits (Core cast traits) for a complete example.
